{"repositoryId":410216858,"availability":"available","full":"harshitsinghai77/server-sent-events-using-fastapi-and-reactjs","name":"server-sent-events-using-fastapi-and-reactjs","description":"Server-Sent Events using FastAPI, Starlette, and ReactJS.","category":"Developer tools","language":"Python","stars":45,"forks":3,"created":"2021-09-25T08:21:49Z","updated":"2021-10-02T08:54:53Z","branch":"master","homepage":"https://fictionally-irrelevant.vercel.app/posts/why-you-should-use-server-side-events-over-web-sockets-and-long-polling","topics":[],"demo":"https://fictionally-irrelevant.vercel.app/posts/why-you-should-use-server-side-events-over-web-sockets-and-long-polling","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T16:55:06.397Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-18T16:55:06.397Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-18T16:55:06.397Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-18T16:55:06.397Z","stars":45,"forks":3}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Demo app to send server-side events from python (fastapi) and receive events using React.","Server Sent Events are a standard allowing browser clients to receive a stream of updates from a server over a HTTP connection without resorting to polling. Unlike WebSockets, Server Sent Events are a one way communications channel - events flow from server to client only.","Server-Sent Events (SSE) is often overshadowed by its two big brothers — Web Sockets and Long-Polling. However, there are many practical use cases for using SSE. Updating dynamic content, sending push notifications, and streaming data in Real-time are just a few of the applications that SSE can be utilized for. We implement a simple SSE application with FastAPI and React."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/harshitsinghai77/server-sent-events-using-fastapi-and-reactjs/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"ac7d9b14e12dd4b4a529349c4a7fcec36872f66aa8e3bf3e8b15c753d3a66bdc","extractedAt":"2026-10-18T16:55:06.502Z"},"overvie